\section*{FPRAS for common integer polymatroid bases}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 114; 2026-10-05), the asserted result is that common integer polymatroid bases with binary capacities can be counted by an FPRAS in rank-oracle and bit complexity polynomial in the encoded data, without expanding capacities into copies.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
On $E=[n]$, let $r_1,r_2:2^E\to\mathbb Z_{\ge0}$ be normalized, nondecreasing submodular functions with $r_1(E)=r_2(E)=R$. Exact value oracles and binary $R$ are supplied. Count each integral vector once in
\[
 \Omega=\{x\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}^E:x(E)=R,\ x(A)\le r_i(A)\ (A\subseteq E,\ i=1,2)\},\qquad Z=|\Omega|.
\]
For rational $\varepsilon,\delta\in(0,1)$ the claimed uniform randomized algorithm returns a nonnegative rational $\widehat Z$ with
\[
 \Pr[(1-\varepsilon)Z\le\widehat Z\le(1+\varepsilon)Z]\ge1-\delta.
\]
Empty intersections always return zero. Both oracle calls and other bit operations are polynomial in $n$, encoding length $\ell$ of $n,R,\varepsilon,\delta$, $\varepsilon^{-1}$ and $\log\delta^{-1}$ on every execution. Reading answers and writing queries are charged; internal oracle evaluation is not.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 114; 2026-10-05), balanced port profiles encode the count with two auxiliary factorial weights. Their quadratic signatures give a transport variance bound; power annealing, capped restarts, and rational volume estimates implement the ratios with bounded fair-bit work.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 114; 2026-10-05).

\subsection{Formal verification}
